UR  - https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.ESEM.2026.67
AB  - AIDev-based comparisons of 669 developer-repository pairs in agent-exposed repositories with 228 control pairs find increased PR-description length and small test-change associations. Apparent review changes in before/after comparisons do not remain significant after comparison with controls. This challenges simple attribution of every review trend to agents, without establishing better delivered software.
N1  - Nonrandom adoption defined by first visible agent PR, not first AI use; selection of persistent contributors and active reviewers, median-split controls and limited matching weaken causal identification. Description length and test-file heuristics do not measure explanation quality or defect prevention. Null review contrasts do not establish equivalence. Reporting questions in the time-window and misattribution summaries are logged for clarification; no replication was run.
